## Why This Is Asked

Assess end-to-end latency across a fan-out path, backpressure resilience, region-aware autoscaling, and observability for a large-scale chat system with moderation integration.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end latency and tail behavior (P99/P99.9)
- Backpressure and queue depth management
- Region-aware autoscaling and load distribution
- Observability: tracing, metrics, dashboards, and alerting
- Fault injection: moderation outages, latency spikes, network partitions

## Code Example

```javascript
// P99 latency measurement sketch
function onMessageSent(event) {
  const t0 = performance.now();
  deliverToRecipients(event);
  const latency = performance.now() - t0;
  recordMetric('e2e_latency_ms', latency);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you model and test regional failover guarantees?
- What telemetry would you add to differentiate moderation latency from delivery latency?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical understanding of GraphQL-specific performance issues, notably nested resolvers and potential N+1 queries, in a beginner-friendly, repeatable way.

## Key Concepts
- GraphQL resolver timings
- N+1 query patterns and detection
- Field-level instrumentation (Apollo tracing)
- Load testing patterns: ramp and spike
- Layer isolation with mocks/replayed traces

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: k6 load test for GraphQL
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const payload = JSON.stringify({ query: "{ user(id:1){ id name posts { id title } } }" });
  const res = http.post('https://api.example.com/graphql', payload, { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
  check(res, { 'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
  sleep(1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect N+1 without changing application code?
- What instrumentation would you add if you could only modify test data?

